To connect these dimensions to process parameters, the plots in Fig. 5 can be examined to understand the trends of d, L, and w with p and β determined by the Rubenchik [50] model. Recall that p represents the ratio of diffusion to laser dwell times, while β represents the ratio of enthalpy input to enthalpy of melting. For low values of p, the melt depth is a much weaker function of β than the melt length. Therefore, changes in β caused by changes to laser power and scanning speed will have a much greater effect on the melt pool dimension responsible for roughness on the upskin compared to the downskin. This result is interesting, showing that to control roughness, the importance of specific parameters varies depending on whether the surface is an upskin or downskin.

